13. Real-world operational scenarios you must support
A production restaurant system must handle situations beyond the normal ordering process.
Situation	Expected system behaviour
Customer changes order	Track modification and send kitchen updates
Customer cancels	Apply cancellation permissions and reverse relevant stock/payment effects
Food unavailable	Temporarily disable item across ordering channels
Customer adds more food	Add items to existing table order or create an additional ticket
Customer splits bill	Allocate amounts across multiple payments
Customer pays partially	Keep outstanding balance visible
Waiter leaves employment	Deactivate account and preserve historical sales and commission
Waiter changes branch	Reassign without losing historical records
Table changes waiter	Preserve original order attribution and track reassignment
Internet fails	Support controlled offline POS operation and synchronization
Kitchen printer fails	Allow display-based preparation and fallback printing
Customer doesn't collect	Flag uncollected pickup orders
Supplier delivers less stock	Record actual received quantity
Stock is damaged	Record wastage with authorization
Payment is reversed	Reconcile and update the relevant order financial status

There is one particularly important rule for waiter attribution:

The person who originally generated the order and the person who eventually served it may be different.

Keep both records. This allows the owner to calculate commissions fairly and understand actual waiter performance.
